## Docs Lifecycle

Docs exist to serve current work. A doc that describes finished or abandoned work is noise — retire it.

---

### 1. Where things live

| Kind | Home | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Standing dev instructions | `CLAUDE.md` | Canonical. `.cursor/rules/*.mdc` mirror parts of it |
| Key / prefix naming | `Naming-conventions.md` | Canonical for DB and option keys |
| Backlog — open tasks, bugs, requirements | `docs/SCOS_module-development-plan.md` | The **only** backlog. No second list anywhere |
| Build plans | `.cursor/plans/` | Temporary. One plan per feature |
| Module spec | `site-essentials/Modules/[Module]/SPEC.md` | Only when the module has non-obvious contracts |
| User-facing how-to | Public docs site (brighterwebsites.com.au/software/) | Not in the repo |
| Retired docs | `docs/history/` (plans → `docs/history/plans/`) | Read-only. Never cite as current |

Do not create new markdown files in the repo root. `README.md`, `CLAUDE.md` and `Naming-conventions.md` are the only root docs.

---

### 2. Before creating a doc

- Could this be a section in an existing doc? Add it there.
- Is it a task, bug or requirement? Add it to the backlog, not a new file.
- Is it a user how-to? It belongs on the public docs site.
- Only a build plan or a module spec justifies a new file.

---

### 3. Retiring a plan or requirements doc

When the work a doc describes ships (PR merged):

1. Verify against the code — never trust the doc's own checkboxes or todo statuses.
2. Move anything still outstanding into the backlog, under the right module section.
3. Move the doc to `docs/history/`.

A doc is never "partially retired". Either it's live, or its remaining items move to the backlog and the doc goes to history.

---

### 4. Keeping live docs honest

- If a change makes a live doc wrong, fix the doc in the same PR.
- Mark backlog items done when the PR merges, not before.
- `docs/` is gitignored (local only). Anything that must survive a lost machine belongs in `CLAUDE.md`, a `.mdc` rule or a tracked `SPEC.md`.
